---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import FloatingNav from '../../components/FloatingNav.astro';
import DocTabs from '../../components/DocTabs.astro';

const repoUrl = 'https://github.com/parleq/parleq-speech';
---

<Layout title="Enterprise SSO — Parleq" description="Back Parleq's cloud access with one corporate OIDC sign-in instead of per-user API keys. Federate into AWS Bedrock (AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity) and Google Cloud Vertex AI (Workforce Identity Federation). Per-IdP playbooks for Okta, Microsoft Entra ID, Keycloak, and generic OIDC.">
  <FloatingNav active="docs" />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 pt-16 sm:pt-20 pb-24">
    <p class="text-sm">
      <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`.replace(/\/$/, '') + '/docs/'} class="text-[color:var(--color-text-subtle)] hover:text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">&larr; Docs</a>
    </p>

    <p class="mt-8 text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[color:var(--color-accent)]">Enterprise &middot; Identity federation</p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 font-display text-4xl leading-[1.1] font-medium tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">
      Enterprise SSO
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[17px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      This page is for IT admins standing up Parleq&rsquo;s LLM cleanup access behind <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">one corporate OIDC sign-in</strong> instead of handing each user an API key. The user signs in once with their company identity provider; Parleq federates that sign-in into your cloud &mdash; AWS Bedrock and/or Google Cloud Vertex AI &mdash; and mints short-lived per-cloud credentials at call time. No long-lived cloud secret is stored on the device.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-grape-200)] bg-[color:var(--color-grape-100)]/20 p-5">
      <p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
        <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Setting this up at home without an IT department?</strong> The same engine works for a single user on a free identity provider (Cognito) or a personal Google account &mdash; with different gotchas. See the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`.replace(/\/$/, '') + '/docs/diy-sso/'} class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">DIY: SSO &amp; Sign-in with Google guide</a> for the path-picker and the burn-an-hour gotchas.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- What you get -->
    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      What you get
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      One engine, several legs. The user runs a single OIDC sign-in (PKCE); Parleq trades the resulting token for short-lived cloud credentials on demand. The properties that matter for a security review:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[7px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-[color:var(--color-grape-400)]" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">No per-user API keys.</strong> Nothing to distribute, store, or rotate per employee &mdash; identity comes from your IdP.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[7px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-[color:var(--color-grape-400)]" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Short-lived cloud credentials, memory only.</strong> Only the refresh token touches the Keychain; the cloud credentials live in process memory and expire on their own.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[7px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-[color:var(--color-grape-400)]" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Fail-closed.</strong> If any leg fails, Parleq pastes the raw on-device transcript rather than falling back to a personal credential &mdash; the cleanup pass is skipped, the dictation is not lost.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[7px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-[color:var(--color-grape-400)]" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">A connection doctor.</strong> Settings &rarr; Company Account runs token-free discovery plus a silent refresh and reports which hop (IdP / token exchange / provider) last succeeded or failed &mdash; the place to confirm a new setup end-to-end.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[7px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-[color:var(--color-grape-400)]" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Per-user audit attribution.</strong> The AWS leg carries the signed-in user&rsquo;s email as the role session name, so CloudTrail attributes each call to the person.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[7px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-[color:var(--color-grape-400)]" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">A revocation caveat.</strong> Offboarding (disabling the user at the IdP) takes effect at the next refresh, but already-issued STS credentials are <em>not</em> revocable and live to their session maximum &mdash; keep the AWS session duration short if you need tight offboarding.</span></li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Overview -->
    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      How it works
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Parleq runs an OIDC authorization-code flow with PKCE in a system web view (no embedded browser, no password ever touches Parleq). The flow yields a refresh token, which Parleq stores in the macOS Keychain and uses to mint short-lived ID tokens on demand. Each cloud leg then trades that ID token for its own credentials:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[7px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-[color:var(--color-grape-400)]" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">AWS Bedrock</strong> &mdash; <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code> exchanges the ID token for temporary STS credentials scoped to an IAM role you control.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[7px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-[color:var(--color-grape-400)]" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Google Cloud Vertex AI</strong> &mdash; Workforce Identity Federation exchanges the ID token for a federated access token used directly as the Vertex bearer.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Two principles bound the data handling:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[7px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-[color:var(--color-grape-400)]" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">What crosses which boundary.</strong> Only OIDC tokens reach the identity provider and the cloud STS endpoints (<code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">sts.&lt;region&gt;.amazonaws.com</code>, <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">sts.googleapis.com</code>) &mdash; never transcript text. Transcript text only ever reaches the cleanup LLM endpoint you already configured, exactly as with any other auth mode.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[7px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-[color:var(--color-grape-400)]" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Fail-closed.</strong> If sign-in, refresh, or the per-cloud exchange fails, Parleq does <em>not</em> silently fall back to a personal credential or a different provider. It fails closed and pastes the raw on-device ASR transcript &mdash; the cleanup pass is skipped, but your dictation is never lost. When the failure is just an expired session, the overlay&rsquo;s sign-in notice is tappable &mdash; sign in right there and Parleq offers to re-clean the transcript you just dictated, no trip to Settings required.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Refresh tokens are rotated where the IdP supports it: Parleq persists each newly-issued refresh token to the Keychain the instant it arrives, before doing anything else, so a rotated token is never lost. The access and ID tokens stay in process memory only. Offboarding (disabling the user at the IdP) takes effect at Parleq&rsquo;s next token refresh; note that already-issued STS credentials are not revocable and live to their session maximum, so keep the AWS session duration short if you need tight offboarding (see the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`.replace(/\/$/, '') + '/docs/managed-configuration/'} class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">managed-configuration reference</a> and <a href={`${repoUrl}/blob/main/docs/SECURITY_REVIEW.md`} class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">security review</a>).
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-grape-200)] bg-[color:var(--color-grape-100)]/15 p-5">
      <p class="text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
        <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Azure OpenAI note:</strong> the Azure OpenAI provider keeps its existing Microsoft Entra ID auth (via <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">az login</code>). OIDC federation for Azure is not part of this release &mdash; the two federated legs today are AWS Bedrock and Google Cloud Vertex AI; Azure OpenAI keeps its existing Entra ID and API-key auth modes.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Enabling it -->
    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      Enabling it in Parleq
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Set the shared OIDC section, then turn on whichever cloud leg(s) you need. All of these can be pinned fleet-wide via MDM &mdash; see the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`.replace(/\/$/, '') + '/docs/managed-configuration/'} class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Enterprise OIDC federation keys</a>. Hand-editing <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">~/.parleq/config.json</code> directly:
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-section-tint)] p-4">
      <pre class="text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)] whitespace-pre overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{
  "oidc": {
    "issuer": "https://acme.okta.com",
    "client_id": "0oaEXAMPLEclientid",
    "scopes": ["openid", "profile", "email", "offline_access"],
    "ephemeral_browser": false
  },
  "aws": {
    "region": "us-east-1",
    "auth_mode": "oidc",
    "role_arn": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/ParleqBedrock",
    "session_duration_seconds": 3600
  },
  "vertex": {
    "project": "my-gcp-project",
    "region": "us-central1",
    "auth_mode": "oidcFederation",
    "workforce_provider": "locations/global/workforcePools/parleq-pool/providers/oidc"
  }
}`}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Then open <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Settings &rarr; Company Account</strong> and sign in. That section shows the signed-in identity and a connection doctor that runs token-free discovery plus a silent refresh and reports each cloud leg&rsquo;s last success/failure &mdash; the place to confirm a new IdP or cloud setup end-to-end.
    </p>

    <!-- Per-IdP playbooks -->
    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      Identity-provider playbooks
    </h2>

    <!-- Tabbed picker: the four IdP playbooks are mutually exclusive. No-JS shows all panels
         (accessible + indexable); the inline script from DocTabs enhances to WAI-ARIA tabs. -->
    <div class="mt-8" data-doctabs>
      <div role="tablist" aria-label="Identity provider" class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <button
          type="button" role="tab" id="tab-okta" aria-controls="panel-okta" aria-selected="true"
          class="doctab group flex flex-col rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] p-5 text-left transition-colors hover:border-[color:var(--color-accent)] aria-selected:border-[color:var(--color-grape)] aria-selected:bg-[color:var(--color-panel-ink)] aria-selected:shadow-[0_10px_30px_-16px_rgba(67,32,95,.45)]"
        >
          <span class="flex items-center gap-2.5">
            <span aria-hidden="true" class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg" style="background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-grape) 12%, transparent); color: var(--color-grape);">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="h-4 w-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 2L4 6v6c0 5 3.6 9.7 8 11 4.4-1.3 8-6 8-11V6z"/></svg>
            </span>
            <span class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Okta</span>
          </span>
          <span class="mt-3 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">Native public app with PKCE; works for both the AWS and GCP federation legs.</span>
          <span class="mt-3 text-[12px] font-medium text-[color:var(--color-grape)]">Custom-scheme redirect · refresh rotation</span>
        </button>
        <button
          type="button" role="tab" id="tab-entra" aria-controls="panel-entra" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"
          class="doctab group flex flex-col rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] p-5 text-left transition-colors hover:border-[color:var(--color-accent)] aria-selected:border-[color:var(--color-grape)] aria-selected:bg-[color:var(--color-panel-ink)] aria-selected:shadow-[0_10px_30px_-16px_rgba(67,32,95,.45)]"
        >
          <span class="flex items-center gap-2.5">
            <span aria-hidden="true" class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg" style="background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-grape) 12%, transparent); color: var(--color-grape);">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="h-4 w-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="4" y="2" width="16" height="20" rx="1"/><path d="M9 22v-4h6v4"/><rect x="8" y="6" width="2" height="2"/><rect x="14" y="6" width="2" height="2"/><rect x="8" y="11" width="2" height="2"/><rect x="14" y="11" width="2" height="2"/></svg>
            </span>
            <span class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Microsoft Entra ID</span>
          </span>
          <span class="mt-3 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">App registration with mandatory loopback redirect; live-validated against AWS Bedrock.</span>
          <span class="mt-3 text-[12px] font-medium text-[color:var(--color-grape)]">Loopback redirect required · live-validated</span>
        </button>
        <button
          type="button" role="tab" id="tab-keycloak" aria-controls="panel-keycloak" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"
          class="doctab group flex flex-col rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] p-5 text-left transition-colors hover:border-[color:var(--color-accent)] aria-selected:border-[color:var(--color-grape)] aria-selected:bg-[color:var(--color-panel-ink)] aria-selected:shadow-[0_10px_30px_-16px_rgba(67,32,95,.45)]"
        >
          <span class="flex items-center gap-2.5">
            <span aria-hidden="true" class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg" style="background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-grape) 12%, transparent); color: var(--color-grape);">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="h-4 w-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="8" cy="14" r="4"/><path d="m14 10 5.5-5.5"/><path d="m19 5 1 1"/><path d="m16 7 1 1"/></svg>
            </span>
            <span class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Keycloak</span>
          </span>
          <span class="mt-3 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">Self-hosted public client with S256 PKCE; dev rig with docker-compose in the repo.</span>
          <span class="mt-3 text-[12px] font-medium text-[color:var(--color-grape)]">Self-hosted · dev rig in repo</span>
        </button>
        <button
          type="button" role="tab" id="tab-generic" aria-controls="panel-generic" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"
          class="doctab group flex flex-col rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] p-5 text-left transition-colors hover:border-[color:var(--color-accent)] aria-selected:border-[color:var(--color-grape)] aria-selected:bg-[color:var(--color-panel-ink)] aria-selected:shadow-[0_10px_30px_-16px_rgba(67,32,95,.45)]"
        >
          <span class="flex items-center gap-2.5">
            <span aria-hidden="true" class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg" style="background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-grape) 12%, transparent); color: var(--color-grape);">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="h-4 w-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg>
            </span>
            <span class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Generic OIDC</span>
          </span>
          <span class="mt-3 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">Any PKCE-compliant OIDC provider with HTTPS issuer and refresh-token support.</span>
          <span class="mt-3 text-[12px] font-medium text-[color:var(--color-grape)]">Any PKCE IdP · HTTPS issuer required</span>
        </button>
      </div>

      <!-- Panel: Okta -->
      <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-okta" aria-labelledby="tab-okta" class="mt-10">
        <h3 class="mt-0 border-l-2 border-[color:var(--color-grape-300)] pl-3 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Okta</h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          Register Parleq as a <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">native / public application</strong> (authorization-code + PKCE, no client secret):
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          <li>&bull; Application type: <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Native</strong>. Grant types: Authorization Code and Refresh Token. PKCE: required.</li>
          <li>&bull; Sign-in redirect URI: <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">parleq-auth://oidc/callback</code>.</li>
          <li>&bull; Scopes: <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">openid profile email offline_access</code> (<code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">offline_access</code> is what gets you a refresh token).</li>
          <li>&bull; Issuer: your org / custom authorization-server URL (e.g. <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">https://acme.okta.com</code>). Client ID: the app&rsquo;s client ID.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Refresh rotation:</strong> Okta rotates refresh tokens and runs replay detection &mdash; reusing an old refresh token after rotation revokes the session. Parleq handles this by persisting the rotated token on receipt, but it means a lost rotated token forces a fresh sign-in. Because Okta is a public issuer with a publicly-fetchable JWKS, it works for both the AWS and GCP legs.
        </p>
      </div><!-- /panel-okta -->

      <!--
        Microsoft Entra ID playbook. Entra ID → AWS Bedrock leg LIVE-VALIDATED 2026-06-08
        (sign-in + dictation through a free tenant). The GCP Vertex-workforce leg reuses the
        validated Okta workforce shape (confirm the oid/sub principal claim per tenant).
        Verified against current Microsoft / AWS / GCP docs:
          Redirect URI rules (https + localhost-http only; custom schemes unsupported for
            apps implementing OAuth directly; "Mobile and desktop applications" platform;
            http-loopback added via the app-manifest replyUrlsWithType; port ignored for
            loopback matching):
            https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/reply-url
            https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/how-to-add-redirect-uri
          v2.0 issuer / discovery / tid+iss+aud claim shape:
            https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/v2-protocols-oidc
            https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/access-tokens
            https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/id-token-claims-reference
          AWS IAM OIDC provider + AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity audience/trust:
            https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/how-to-access-aws-resources-from-microsoft-entra-id-tenants-using-aws-security-token-service/
          GCP Workforce Identity Federation with Entra (issuer, oid/sub attribute mapping,
            web-sso flags):
            https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/workforce-sign-in-microsoft-entra-id
      -->
      <!-- Panel: Microsoft Entra ID -->
      <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-entra" aria-labelledby="tab-entra" class="mt-10">
        <h3 class="mt-0 border-l-2 border-[color:var(--color-grape-300)] pl-3 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Microsoft Entra ID</h3>
        <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-grape-200)] bg-[color:var(--color-grape-100)]/20 p-4">
          <p class="text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
            <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Entra requires the loopback redirect.</strong> Unlike Okta/Cognito (custom scheme) or Google (either), Entra rejects custom-scheme redirects for apps that implement OAuth directly &mdash; so Parleq&rsquo;s loopback sign-in (default browser + a transient <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">127.0.0.1</code> listener) is mandatory here, not optional. Register the redirect via the app manifest&rsquo;s <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">publicClient.redirectUris</code> (the portal&rsquo;s &ldquo;Web&rdquo; platform is the wrong type). The Entra&nbsp;ID&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;AWS&nbsp;Bedrock leg below is live-validated; the GCP&nbsp;Vertex leg uses the same workforce-federation shape as the validated Okta leg (confirm the <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oid</code> vs <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">sub</code> principal claim against your tenant).
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          Register Parleq under <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">App registrations &rarr; New registration</strong>. Pick <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">single-tenant</strong> (&ldquo;Accounts in this organizational directory only&rdquo;) for a corporate deployment &mdash; it pins the token issuer to your tenant GUID and is the form both cloud legs federate against most cleanly:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Redirect URI:</strong> add a <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Mobile and desktop applications</strong> platform (<em>not</em> the iOS/macOS platform &mdash; that one is MSAL-only and forces the <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">msauth.&lt;bundle-id&gt;://auth</code> scheme, which Parleq&rsquo;s own OIDC engine doesn&rsquo;t emit). Entra accepts only <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">https</code> and loopback-<code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">http</code> redirects for an app implementing OAuth directly &mdash; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">a custom scheme like <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">parleq-auth://oidc/callback</code> is rejected.</strong> Register <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">http://127.0.0.1/oidc/callback</code> &mdash; the <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">path must match</strong> the <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">redirect_uri</code> path you set in Parleq&rsquo;s config (below). Entra ignores only the <em>port</em> when matching loopback redirects (not the path), and Parleq always sends the configured path with a kernel-chosen ephemeral port, so <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">http://127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;/oidc/callback</code> matches the registered <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">http://127.0.0.1/oidc/callback</code>. The portal&rsquo;s redirect-URI text box may refuse an <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">http</code> loopback value &mdash; if so, add it via the app manifest&rsquo;s <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">replyUrlsWithType</code> (type <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">InstalledClient</code>).</li>
          <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Public client:</strong> no client secret. Under <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Authentication &rarr; Advanced settings</strong>, &ldquo;Allow public client flows&rdquo; can stay at its default; PKCE is automatic for the auth-code flow.</li>
          <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Scopes:</strong> <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">openid profile email offline_access</code> (<code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">offline_access</code> is what yields a refresh token). These are delegated, user-consentable permissions; if your tenant requires admin consent, grant it once on the app registration so users aren&rsquo;t prompted.</li>
          <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Issuer:</strong> <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">https://login.microsoftonline.com/&lt;tenant-id&gt;/v2.0</code> (the <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">v2.0</strong> endpoint &mdash; not the v1.0 <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">sts.windows.net</code> form). Its discovery doc lives at <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">.../v2.0/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>. Tokens carry an <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">iss</code> of exactly that form with your tenant GUID, a matching <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">tid</code> claim, and an <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">aud</code> equal to the app&rsquo;s Application (client) ID. <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Client ID:</strong> the registration&rsquo;s Application (client) ID.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">Parleq config &mdash; note the loopback <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">redirect_uri</code>, which routes sign-in through the default-browser + transient-127.0.0.1-listener path instead of the custom-scheme web view:</p>
        <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-section-tint)] p-4">
          <pre class="text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)] whitespace-pre overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{
  "oidc": {
    "issuer": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0",
    "client_id": "<application-client-id>",
    "scopes": ["openid", "profile", "email", "offline_access"],
    "redirect_uri": "http://127.0.0.1/oidc/callback",
    "ephemeral_browser": false
  }
}`}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Conditional access &amp; device trust:</strong> Parleq signs in through the system browser, which shares the Safari session &mdash; so a device already enrolled / compliant in Entra carries that state into the sign-in, and conditional-access policies (MFA, compliant-device, managed-network) apply as usual without Parleq handling any of it. <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Refresh rotation:</strong> Entra issues rotating refresh tokens; Parleq persists each rotated token on receipt, so a normal refresh is silent and a lost rotated token forces a fresh sign-in. Because the v2.0 issuer is public with a publicly-fetchable JWKS, Entra works for both the AWS and GCP legs.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          The two cloud legs reuse the same IAM-OIDC-provider / role-trust and Workforce-pool steps as the other IdPs (see <a href="#aws-bedrock-setup" class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">AWS Bedrock setup</a> and <a href="#google-cloud-vertex-ai-setup" class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Google Cloud Vertex AI setup</a> below), with the Entra-specific values noted here. For the AWS leg, the IAM OIDC provider URL is the v2.0 issuer <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">https://login.microsoftonline.com/&lt;tenant-id&gt;/v2.0</code> and the role&rsquo;s trust-policy <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">aud</code> condition is the Application (client) ID:
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-section-tint)] p-4">
          <pre class="text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)] whitespace-pre overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Principal": {
    "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::<account>:oidc-provider/login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0"
  },
  "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
  "Condition": {
    "StringEquals": {
      "login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0:aud": "<application-client-id>"
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          Set <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">aws.role_arn</code> to the role (e.g. <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">arn:aws:iam::&lt;account&gt;:role/ParleqDictation</code>) and <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">aws.auth_mode</code> to <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidc</code>; the permissions policy and per-region model-access notes in the AWS section apply unchanged.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          For the GCP leg, create the Workforce Identity Federation OIDC provider with the same issuer and the Application (client) ID as the allowed audience. Entra v2.0 tokens federate as a <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">web-SSO</strong> provider; map the subject from Entra&rsquo;s immutable object-ID claim and request the auth-code response type:
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-section-tint)] p-4">
          <pre class="text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)] whitespace-pre overflow-x-auto"><code>{`gcloud iam workforce-pools providers create-oidc entra \\
  --workforce-pool="<pool>" \\
  --location="global" \\
  --issuer-uri="https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0" \\
  --client-id="<application-client-id>" \\
  --attribute-mapping="google.subject=assertion.oid,google.display_name=assertion.name" \\
  --web-sso-response-type="code" \\
  --web-sso-assertion-claims-behavior="merge-user-info-over-id-token-claims"`}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Pick the federated principal claim carefully.</strong> Entra&rsquo;s <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oid</code> (object ID) is the stable, immutable per-user GUID within a tenant and is the recommended subject; <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">sub</code> is pairwise (per-app) and also stable but app-scoped. Use <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">assertion.oid</code> unless you have a specific reason to scope to <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">sub</code>. Then put the provider&rsquo;s full resource name (<code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">locations/global/workforcePools/&lt;pool&gt;/providers/entra</code>) in <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertex.workforce_provider</code>, set <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertex.auth_mode</code> to <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidcFederation</code>, and follow the rest of the <a href="#google-cloud-vertex-ai-setup" class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Vertex AI setup</a> (role binding, <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">x-goog-user-project</code>).
        </p>
      </div><!-- /panel-entra -->

      <!-- Panel: Keycloak -->
      <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-keycloak" aria-labelledby="tab-keycloak" class="mt-10">
        <h3 class="mt-0 border-l-2 border-[color:var(--color-grape-300)] pl-3 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Keycloak</h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          Create a <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">public client</strong> in your realm with the standard (authorization-code) flow enabled and PKCE (S256) required:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          <li>&bull; Client authentication: <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">off</strong> (public client). Standard flow: on. PKCE method: <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">S256</code>.</li>
          <li>&bull; Valid redirect URI: <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">parleq-auth://oidc/callback</code>.</li>
          <li>&bull; Realm refresh rotation: <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">revokeRefreshToken</code> on with <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">refreshTokenMaxReuse</code> 0 to rotate on every refresh.</li>
          <li>&bull; Issuer: <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">https://&lt;host&gt;/realms/&lt;realm&gt;</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          A ready-made dev rig &mdash; docker-compose Keycloak with this exact client and a test user &mdash; lives in the repo at <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">scripts/dev/keycloak/</code>. One caveat for end-to-end cloud testing: AWS STS fetches the issuer&rsquo;s JWKS from its own side, so a <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">localhost</code> issuer can&rsquo;t pass <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code>. GCP accepts an inline-uploaded JWKS (no fetch), but requires the issuer URI itself to use HTTPS &mdash; so a plain-http localhost issuer works for neither cloud leg. For live cloud tests, use a hosted free issuer (Cognito or an Okta Integrator org) or front Keycloak with a stable-hostname HTTPS tunnel.
        </p>
      </div><!-- /panel-keycloak -->

      <!-- Panel: Generic OIDC -->
      <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-generic" aria-labelledby="tab-generic" class="mt-10">
        <h3 class="mt-0 border-l-2 border-[color:var(--color-grape-300)] pl-3 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Generic OIDC</h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          Any spec-compliant OIDC provider works if it offers:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
          <li>&bull; A discovery document at <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">&lt;issuer&gt;/.well-known/openid-configuration</code> with <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">authorization_endpoint</code> and <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">token_endpoint</code> (a <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">revocation_endpoint</code> enables clean sign-out).</li>
          <li>&bull; A public client (no secret) supporting authorization-code + PKCE (S256).</li>
          <li>&bull; The custom-scheme redirect URI <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">parleq-auth://oidc/callback</code>.</li>
          <li>&bull; Refresh tokens (request <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">offline_access</code>) so Parleq can refresh silently.</li>
          <li>&bull; An HTTPS issuer URI; the AWS leg additionally needs the JWKS publicly fetchable (GCP can take an inline-uploaded JWKS instead).</li>
        </ul>
      </div><!-- /panel-generic -->
    </div><!-- /data-doctabs -->

    <!-- AWS setup -->
    <h2 id="aws-bedrock-setup" class="mt-12 scroll-mt-20 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      AWS Bedrock setup
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      In IAM, register the IdP as an <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">OpenID Connect identity provider</strong> (the issuer URL plus the client ID as an audience), then create a role whose trust policy admits tokens from that provider. <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code> is the gate: the call itself is unauthenticated, so the role&rsquo;s trust policy is what authorizes access &mdash; constrain it with an <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">aud</code> condition.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">Example role trust policy (placeholder account ID and client ID):</p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-section-tint)] p-4">
      <pre class="text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)] whitespace-pre overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/acme.okta.com"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "acme.okta.com:aud": "0oaEXAMPLEclientid"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">Attach a permissions policy granting Bedrock model invocation:</p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-section-tint)] p-4">
      <pre class="text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)] whitespace-pre overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["bedrock:InvokeModel", "bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream"],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">"Resource": "*"</code> keeps the example short &mdash; scope it to the specific model or inference-profile ARNs you have approved for least privilege.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Put the role&rsquo;s ARN in <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">aws.role_arn</code> and set <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">aws.auth_mode</code> to <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidc</code>. The requested STS session duration is <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">aws.session_duration_seconds</code> (default 3600, clamped to 900&ndash;43200). Bedrock model access is per-region, so make sure the chosen region has the model enabled. The role&rsquo;s session name carries the signed-in user&rsquo;s email for CloudTrail attribution.
    </p>

    <!-- GCP setup -->
    <h2 id="google-cloud-vertex-ai-setup" class="mt-12 scroll-mt-20 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      Google Cloud Vertex AI setup
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Create a <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Workforce Identity Pool</strong> and add an <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">OIDC provider</strong> for your IdP (issuer URL + the client ID as the allowed audience). For a publicly-reachable issuer GCP fetches the JWKS automatically; for a non-public issuer you can upload the JWKS inline (<code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">jwks_json</code>) &mdash; but note the issuer URI must use HTTPS either way. Workforce Identity Pools are an Organization-level resource &mdash; creating one requires a GCP Organization (Cloud Identity) and <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">iam.workforcePools.create</code>, not just project access.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Attribute mapping:</strong> <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">google.subject = assertion.sub</code>.</li>
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Grant access:</strong> bind <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">roles/aiplatform.user</code> to the workforce-pool principal set so federated identities can call Vertex AI. Scope the binding tighter than the whole pool if you can (e.g. by group attribute).</li>
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Workforce provider name:</strong> put the full resource name in <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertex.workforce_provider</code> (<code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">locations/global/workforcePools/&lt;pool&gt;/providers/&lt;provider&gt;</code>) and set <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertex.auth_mode</code> to <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidcFederation</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Workforce-federated calls must specify a billing/quota project: Parleq sends your configured <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertex.project</code> as the <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">x-goog-user-project</code> header on every Vertex request, so that project needs the Vertex AI API enabled and the federated principal needs <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">serviceusage.services.use</code> on it.
    </p>

    <!-- Google account -> Vertex directly -->
    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      Google account &rarr; Vertex AI directly
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      The third leg skips federation entirely. GCP refuses to accept Google itself as a workforce IdP, so the workforce-pool path can&rsquo;t do &ldquo;sign in with Google, dictate with Gemini.&rdquo; Instead, Parleq&rsquo;s own Google sign-in requests the <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">cloud-platform</code> scope, and the resulting OAuth <em>access</em> token is a valid Vertex bearer directly &mdash; exactly what gcloud Application Default Credentials produces, done in-app, with no broker and no gcloud install. This is the <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">googleOAuth</code> Vertex mode.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      <li>&bull; In the Google Cloud console, create an <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">OAuth client ID of the Desktop app type</strong> (public, no usable secret for this flow) and set <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidc.redirect_uri</code> to <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">http://127.0.0.1/oauth2redirect</code> &mdash; Google&rsquo;s current desktop guidance. Parleq signs in via the default browser and answers the callback on a temporary <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">127.0.0.1</strong> listener (ephemeral port; present only during sign-in). <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Alternate:</strong> an <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">iOS-type</strong> client also works &mdash; use its reversed-client-ID redirect (<code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">com.googleusercontent.apps.&lt;client-id&gt;:/oauth2redirect</code>), which Parleq intercepts in-process with no local listener.</li>
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Admin note (managed Macs):</strong> the loopback sign-in only activates for an <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">http://127.0.0.1</code> redirect. Pin <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidcRedirectURI</code> to your custom-scheme value (e.g. <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">parleq-auth://oidc/callback</code> or the iOS-type reversed-client-ID scheme) to ensure Parleq never binds a local listener. The redirect URI is the complete control &mdash; there is no separate &ldquo;disable loopback&rdquo; key because pinning a custom-scheme redirect already prevents the listener from ever activating.</li>
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Scopes:</strong> <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">["openid", "email", "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform"]</code> &mdash; the <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">cloud-platform</code> scope is what makes the access token usable against Vertex.</li>
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Force a refresh token:</strong> set <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidc.extra_auth_params</code> to <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">{`{"access_type": "offline", "prompt": "select_account consent"}`}</code>. Google only re-issues a refresh token when it shows the consent screen.</li>
      <li>&bull; Set <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertex.auth_mode</code> to <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">googleOAuth</code> and <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertex.project</code> to the GCP project that owns the Vertex AI quota. Every call carries <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">x-goog-user-project</code>, so the signing account needs both <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">roles/aiplatform.user</code> and <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">roles/serviceusage.serviceUsageConsumer</code> on it (a project Owner already has both).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      For org-owned Google identities this works the same way &mdash; with no &ldquo;unverified app&rdquo; warning and no refresh-token cap if the OAuth client lives in your Google Cloud organization. The personal-@gmail variant, with its consent-screen trade-offs and the granular-consent gotcha, is covered in the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`.replace(/\/$/, '') + '/docs/diy-sso/'} class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">DIY guide</a>.
    </p>

    <!-- MDM key reference pointer -->
    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      Pin it fleet-wide
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Every value above can be pushed via MDM so users sign in but can&rsquo;t re-point the app at a personal tenant. The nine enterprise OIDC keys &mdash; <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidcIssuer</code>, <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidcClientID</code>, <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidcScopes</code>, <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidcRedirectURI</code>, <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidcExtraAuthParams</code>, <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidcEphemeralBrowserSession</code>, <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">awsRoleArn</code>, <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">awsSessionDurationSeconds</code>, and <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertexWorkforceProvider</code> &mdash; sit alongside the existing managed-config surface. Enable a leg by also pinning <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">awsAuthMode: oidc</code> and/or <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertexAuthMode: oidcFederation</code> (for Workforce federation) or <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertexAuthMode: googleOAuth</code> (for the Sign-in-with-Google direct leg). See the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`.replace(/\/$/, '') + '/docs/managed-configuration/'} class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Managed Configuration reference &rarr; Enterprise OIDC federation</a> for the full table, and the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`.replace(/\/$/, '') + '/docs/admin-guide/'} class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Admin Guide</a> for the deployment workflow.
    </p>

    <!-- Troubleshooting -->
    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      Troubleshooting
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Start at <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Settings &rarr; Company Account &rarr; Test connection</strong>. The connection doctor reports which hop failed (IdP discovery / token exchange / cloud provider) with the server&rsquo;s reason, which usually points straight at the fix below.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)]">
      <table class="w-full text-[14px] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
        <thead class="border-b border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-surface)]">
          <tr>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Symptom</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Cause / fix</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-[color:var(--color-border)]">
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="font-mono text-[12px]">tokenCacheNotFound</code> / SSO token error on AWS</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">If your IAM OIDC provider URL or the IdP issuer ends in <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">/#</code>, drop the trailing <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">#</code> &mdash; the INI parser strips it mid-value. Confirm the issuer in the doctor matches the IAM OIDC provider exactly.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-surface)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="font-mono text-[12px]">AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code> rejected (audience / not authorized)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">The role&rsquo;s trust policy <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">aud</code> condition must equal the OIDC client ID, and the <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">Federated</code> principal must name the same issuer host you registered. The STS call is unauthenticated &mdash; the trust policy is the only gate.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Entra sign-in fails immediately (<code class="font-mono text-[12px]">AADSTS50011</code> redirect mismatch)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">The redirect URI Parleq sends isn&rsquo;t registered. Entra rejects custom schemes for an app implementing OAuth directly &mdash; set <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">oidc.redirect_uri</code> to <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">http://127.0.0.1/oidc/callback</code> and register the same value <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">http://127.0.0.1/oidc/callback</code> under the <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Mobile and desktop applications</strong> platform (via the manifest&rsquo;s <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">replyUrlsWithType</code> if the portal text box refuses http-loopback). Entra ignores the port for loopback matching but still compares the path, so the registered path must match.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-surface)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3">AWS leg fails with a JWKS / issuer error on a self-hosted IdP</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">STS fetches the issuer&rsquo;s JWKS server-side, so a <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">localhost</code> issuer cannot pass. Front the IdP with a stable-hostname HTTPS URL (or use a hosted issuer such as Cognito).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3">GCP workforce provider create fails: &ldquo;issuer must be HTTPS&rdquo;</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">GCP can take an inline-uploaded JWKS (no fetch), but the issuer URI itself must still be HTTPS &mdash; a plain-http localhost issuer works for neither cloud leg.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-surface)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Sign-in window flashes open and closes (<code class="font-mono text-[12px]">invalid_scope</code>)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">The IdP rejected a requested scope the client doesn&rsquo;t have enabled. On a Cognito app client this is usually <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">profile</code> &mdash; request only <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">["openid", "email"]</code> or enable Profile in the app client. Cognito also rejects <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">offline_access</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Signed out shortly after signing in (refresh rejected)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">A rotated refresh token was lost, or the IdP enforces replay detection. Sign in again (from Settings &rarr; Company Account, or by tapping the overlay&rsquo;s sign-in notice after a dictation); for IdPs that rotate on every refresh, ensure nothing else is reusing the token.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <DocTabs />
